{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Under Rule 6c-11, a US ETF relying on the rule must make these disclosures publicly available, free of charge, on its website:\n\n- **Portfolio holdings:** Before the regular trading session opens on each business day, disclose the prior-close portfolio holdings that will form the basis for the next NAV calculation. These are the ETF’s actual portfolio investments—not merely its creation/redemption basket.\n- **Daily price and NAV:** Each business day, show the prior business day’s closing market price and NAV, and the premium or discount of that market price to NAV. Keep the prior-year history available; the rule’s website requirement is for at least one year.\n- **Premium/discount history:** If the ETF traded at a premium or discount for more than 2% of its trading days over a rolling 30-calendar-day period, explain that fact and provide a line graph of the premiums/discounts for the most recent one-year period. Update the explanation and graph as required by the rule.\n- **Bid–ask spread:** Show the median bid–ask spread over the most recent 30 calendar days, updated at least quarterly.\n\n**Transaction baskets are different:** the rule does not require public posting of each day’s creation/redemption basket. It does require written basket-construction and acceptance procedures, including policies for custom baskets, but those are not a substitute for the portfolio-holdings disclosure."}}
